# Embr Cursor Rules

## What Embr Is
A done-for-you live event/trip guide. Every guide is a single `TripConfig` (zod schema, `packages/hub-app/src/types/blocks-schema.ts`) rendered by one block engine (`BlockRenderer` + one view component per block type). There is no per-client code, no industry registry, no native app. Full detail: `EMBR_KNOWLEDGE_LEDGER.md`. Visual rules: `DESIGN.md`.

## Session Start
1. Read `EMBR_KNOWLEDGE_LEDGER.md` for current architecture/status.
2. Read `DEV_LOG.md` for recent history.
3. Optionally run `npm run session:init` (git sync, dependency check, quality checks).

Dev log: `npm run devlog:update` ensures today's entry exists in `DEV_LOG.md`; `npm run devlog:append -- "message"` adds a bullet to it.

## Config & Rendering Conventions
- Firestore (`client-configs/{clientId}`) is the source of truth for a live guide; `packages/hub-app/public/client-configs/*.json` is the static fallback + local authoring output.
- Every config must validate against `TripConfigSchema` before it's pushed. Run `npm run client:validate` or `node scripts/configs-push.js --dry`.
- A new block type means: extend the schema in `blocks-schema.ts`, add a view component under `renderers/blocks/`, wire it into `BlockRenderer.tsx`. There is no other place client-specific logic lives.
- Guide theming (`config.theme.colors.*`) is scoped entirely inside the guide-render path (`ClientApp` → `BlockRenderer` → block views) and must never leak into the hub shell (`AccessCodeEntry.tsx`, `LoadingScreen.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `page.tsx`, `globals.css` — these keep fixed hub colors).

## Design Compliance
Check any guide-render-path change against `DESIGN.md`'s actual rules — border-only elevation (no shadow/glow/gradient), no emoji/em dashes/hype in interface copy, Geist Mono for time/date/status, 8px spacing grid, mobile nav fixed at the bottom vs. desktop nav in the header, 2px focus ring with 3px offset. Token-compatibility (does it read `config.theme.colors.*`) is a different, narrower check than DESIGN.md compliance — both matter.

EmbrKit (`@embr/ui`) components are a starting point, not a mandate. Where shared EmbrKit CSS conflicts with DESIGN.md (card shadows, default emoji icons), prefer a small local primitive scoped to the guide-render path (see `GuideCard.tsx`) over editing shared styles that affect unrelated consumers.

## Authoring a Guide
Normal flow: the `import-guide` Claude Code skill (`.claude/skills/import-guide/SKILL.md`). By hand: `npm run client:create` to scaffold, `npm run client:validate`, then `npm run configs:push:one -- <slug>`.

## Quality Gates
- `npm run audit:theme` — no hardcoded colors in the guide-render path.
- `npm run lint`, `npm run test` (Vitest) before opening a PR.
- Manual 30s keyboard-focus pass on anything touching the guide-render path.
- Hub shell visuals unchanged unless explicitly intended.

## Git Workflow (optional but available)
```bash
npm run git:session <name>    # Create a session branch
npm run git:save "message"    # Commit + push progress
npm run git:end                # Merge session branch to main, clean up
npm run git:status             # Check current branch/status
```
Never work directly on `main` for anything non-trivial.

## What Not to Reintroduce
These belonged to an earlier, retired direction — don't rebuild them without a real reason (see `EMBR_KNOWLEDGE_LEDGER.md`'s History section):
- Per-client React components / an industry-folder registry / a lazy-loader map.
- A native app via Capacitor, or QR-code-only distribution.
- A template-showcase gallery or standalone-app generator.
